# NO TRAILING SLASH: in a worktree, node_modules is a SYMLINK to the main checkout's copy, and
# `node_modules/` matches a directory only. A `git add -A` in a worktree committed the symlink on
# 2 September 2026, which would point every other checkout at one machine's home directory.
node_modules

# Vite's pre-bundled dependencies, one cache per checkout. It used to live in node_modules, which
# in a worktree is a symlink to the main checkout's copy, so every checkout wrote into one cache
# and the owner's running dev server read it. vite.config.ts has the account. Rebuilt in about a
# second by the next `npm run dev`.
.cache/

dist/
data/
*.db
*.db-journal
*.db-wal
*.db-shm
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
.DS_Store
*.log

# Generated screenshots. Regenerable with tools/shot.mjs and the design mockups themselves,
# and 61 MB of the 63 MB in this tree. The design/*/index.html files ARE kept — they are the
# work; their renders are not.
tools/shots/
tools/review/
_judge_shots/
_viz_demo/
design/*/preview.png

# The same rule, applied to the UI redesign exploration. design/shots/ alone is 357 MB, because
# these prototypes are review documents that run 20,000 to 40,000 pixels tall and there are four
# pictures of each. Rebuild any of them in seconds:
#
#   node design/shot.mjs design/concepts/E-console/index.html   # one prototype
#   node design/sheet.mjs                                       # the contact sheet over them all
#
# What IS kept is every prototype's index.html and RATIONALE.md, the audits, the fixture and the
# proposals. Those are the work. A 7 MB picture of a page whose source sits beside it is not, and
# a repository that is going to be made public should not carry a third of a gigabyte of them.
# No picture anywhere under design/ is source. Every one of them is rendered from an .html file
# sitting beside it, including the crops and slices agents made while reading their own work.
design/shots/
design/**/*.png
design/index.html

# ...except the avatar set, which is not a picture of a page. `design/avatars/png/*.png` is what an
# upload actually sends: the avatar route takes png, jpeg and webp and refuses SVG, so the drawing
# beside it cannot be the thing uploaded. Each is about 30 KB, not 7 MB, and
# tests/the-avatar-set-is-complete.test.ts names every one of them, so a checkout without them is a
# checkout with a failing suite.
!design/avatars/png/*.png

# And the generated avatars, which are source in the strongest sense: nothing in this repository can
# remake them. Each came from one paid call to an image model on OpenRouter, asking again costs money
# and returns a different picture, and there is no .html file beside them to render.
!design/avatars/generated/*.png

# And the candidates for the round chat button, for the same reason: each came from a paid call and
# nothing here can remake it. previews.html beside them is the page that shows them at the size the
# button is actually drawn at, and it is the .html this rule's reason above asks every picture to sit
# beside -- except that here the pictures are the source and the page is what is rendered from them.
!design/avatars/circle/*.png

# Python bytecode from a design build script
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# Throwaway probe scripts. An adversarial reviewer writes these while a commit is being made,
# and eight of them were once swept into one by a stage-everything command.
tools/.*

.logs/
.claude/worktrees/
.tmp-r10/
.probe/

# The rules file Codex and OpenCode read. It is GENERATED, by src/worker/rules.ts, from CLAUDE.md
# and the Mole Office skill, into the working directory of a run just before that run starts. It is
# not a source: editing one would be edited over by the next run, and committing one would be a
# second copy of the rules free to drift from the first. There is one source and one writer, and
# tests/one-set-of-rules.test.ts holds all three of those facts.
AGENTS.md

# The role card portraits (P47) are kept, like the drawn avatars
!design/portraits/**/*.png

# Personal custom portraits stay on this machine and are not uploaded.
data/portraits/

# The note the secretary leaves a builder with the answers to the forks in its brief. It is a message,
# not product content, and every builder that gets one would otherwise commit it.
# A brief a coordinating agent leaves in a builder's worktree. THEIR-ANSWERS.md is where the secretary wrote
# down the answers to the two criteria a build was waiting on, so the builder could read them
# without a round trip. It is a message, not a document of this repository: the person's messages belong
# in design/messages/ACCEPTANCE.md, where the criteria they settle are, and a second copy in the root
# is one free to drift from that. It is ignored rather than only deleted because `git add -A` brought
# it back once after it had been removed from the index.
THEIR-ANSWERS.md

# worker scratch homes (one per worktree; 16 September 2026)
.scratch/

# Three inventories a tool writes from the tree. They are kept out of git (17 November 2026), so no change has to
# carry them and the merge queue has nothing to join or rewrite. To have one, run its tool: `npm run ownership` in
# plugins/office-map/, `node tools/test-audit-table.mjs`, and `node tests/server-refusal-inventory.ts --write`.
plugins/office-map/OWNERSHIP.md
design/ground/TEST-AUDIT-2026-09-03.md
tests/server-refusals-without-codes.json
